      <div set:html={`
<p>For years, Amazon treated EPUB like a competitor. If you wanted to read a Kindle book on any device that wasn't a Kindle or a Kindle app, you needed third-party tools, conversion workarounds, and a degree in digital forensics. The company's download options were limited to proprietary formats — MOBI, AZW, KFX — or the e-ink-only PRC.</p>

<p>That changed in December 2025. Amazon quietly began allowing <strong>EPUB and PDF downloads for DRM-free books published through Kindle Direct Publishing (KDP)</strong>. As of June 2026, this feature is live, documented, and increasingly critical as Amazon sunsets its legacy tools — the Download &amp; Transfer via USB option is already gone, and Kindle for PC shuts down on June 30.</p>

<p>This article explains <strong>what Amazon changed, how to download your KDP books as EPUB, what formats you can actually get, and what this means for the broader ebook format landscape.</strong></p>

<hr />

<h2 id="what-changed">What Changed: The KDP DRM Policy Shift</h2>

<p>The shift has two parts, and understanding both is essential.</p>

<p><strong>Part one: Amazon made DRM opt-in for KDP titles.</strong> In December 2025, Amazon announced that Kindle Direct Publishing authors could choose whether to apply DRM (Digital Rights Management) to their books. Previously, DRM was the default — almost all KDP titles were locked to Amazon's ecosystem. Now, authors can publish DRM-free by default, and approximately 30–40% of new KDP titles are DRM-free as of mid-2026, according to independent estimates.</p>

<p><strong>Part two: DRM-free KDP books can be downloaded as EPUB or PDF.</strong> If a book has no DRM, Amazon's KDP dashboard presents a download button with EPUB and PDF options alongside the traditional Kindle format. This is the first time Amazon has officially offered EPUB as a download format for its catalog — previously, EPUB was only available through third-party tools or by converting from AZW/MOBI.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Before Dec 2025</th>
      <th>After Dec 2025</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>DRM applied to ~95% of KDP titles by default</td>
      <td>DRM is opt-in; ~30-40% of new titles are DRM-free</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>No EPUB download option at all</td>
      <td>EPUB and PDF downloads available for DRM-free titles</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Download &amp; Transfer via USB was the only export path</td>
      <td>USB transfer removed; EPUB/PDF downloads fill the gap</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Readers needed Calibre + DeDRM to liberate their library</td>
      <td>DRM-free titles need zero conversion tools</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>This is not a full reversal — Amazon still applies DRM to traditionally published books from the Big Five publishers and any KDP author who opts in. But for the growing segment of indie-published DRM-free content, the EPUB download is a genuinely new option.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="how-to-download">How to Download KDP Books as EPUB (Step-by-Step)</h2>

<p>The process is simple, but it's not immediately obvious from Amazon's main product pages. Here's how to find and use the EPUB download option.</p>

<h3 id="step-1-find-a-drm-free-book">Step 1: Find a DRM-Free Book</h3>
<p>You cannot determine DRM status from the product page alone. The easiest way to identify DRM-free books is to check your <strong>Amazon Content &amp; Devices</strong> page (formerly Manage Your Content and Devices). Books with no DRM will show a download icon that reveals multiple format options when clicked.</p>

<h3 id="step-2-access-the-kdp-dashboard">Step 2: Access the KDP Dashboard</h3>
<p>If you are the <strong>author or publisher</strong> of a KDP title:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Log into <strong>kdp.amazon.com</strong></li>
  <li>Navigate to your <strong>Bookshelf</strong></li>
  <li>Click the ellipsis (<strong>...</strong>) menu next to your book</li>
  <li>Select <strong>Download eBook</strong></li>
  <li>Choose between <strong>EPUB (.epub)</strong>, <strong>PDF (.pdf)</strong>, or <strong>Kindle Format (.mobi or .kpf)</strong></li>
</ol>

<h3 id="step-3-read-anywhere">Step 3: Read Anywhere</h3>
<p>Once you have the EPUB file, you can:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Upload it to <strong>Google Play Books</strong> for cross-device sync</li>
  <li>Transfer it to a <strong>Kobo</strong>, <strong>PocketBook</strong>, or <strong>Tolino</strong> e-reader</li>
  <li>Open it in <strong>Apple Books</strong>, <strong>Thorium Reader</strong>, or <strong>Calibre</strong></li>
  <li>Convert it to another format using <strong>converter-epub.com</strong> or <strong>Calibre</strong></li>
  <li>Archive it as a permanent, unencrypted backup</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Note for readers (not authors):</strong> If you <em>bought</em> a DRM-free KDP book, the EPUB download option appears in your Content &amp; Devices page under the same ellipsis menu. Amazon does not promote this option — it's hidden in the settings UI — but it works.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="why-this-matters">Why This Matters: Amazon's Relationship with EPUB</h2>

<p>Amazon's history with EPUB is, to put it gently, complicated. The company has never offered native EPUB reading on any Kindle device. When Amazon launched the Kindle in 2007, it used the closed MOBI format. Over the years, it created AZW, AZW3 (KF8), and KFX — each proprietary, each incompatible with the open EPUB standard that literally every other ebook retailer uses.</p>

<p><strong>Why the shift now? Three factors:</strong></p>

<h3 id="factor-1-regulatory-pressure">1. Regulatory Pressure</h3>
<p>The European Union's Digital Markets Act (DMA) has been pushing platform companies toward interoperability and data portability. While Amazon has argued that Kindle is not a "gatekeeper" platform under the DMA (since it's not an operating system or app store), the regulatory environment has made proprietary lock-in riskier. Offering EPUB downloads for DRM-free content is a low-risk way to demonstrate openness without fundamentally changing the Kindle business model.</p>

<h3 id="factor-2-kdp-author-revolt">2. KDP Author Pressure</h3>
<p>Self-published authors have been increasingly vocal about wanting direct EPUB access. Many authors need EPUB files to submit to Kobo, Apple Books, Google Play Books, and other retailers — and Amazon's KDP platform, despite being the largest distribution channel, was the only one that <em>didn't</em> give its authors native EPUB downloads. The shift was driven as much by author demand as by Amazon's strategic calculation.</p>

<h3 id="factor-3-tool-sunsetting">3. The Tool Sunsetting Coordination</h3>
<p>Amazon removed Download &amp; Transfer via USB in 2025, and Kindle for PC shuts down June 30, 2026. Both moves reduced readers' ability to back up their Kindle libraries. By simultaneously introducing EPUB downloads for DRM-free titles, Amazon provides an alternative escape hatch — specifically for the segment of content where DRM isn't a factor. This timing isn't coincidental.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="limitations">Limitations You Should Know About</h2>

<p>The new EPUB download option is real, but it's not a complete solution. Here are the boundaries:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>DRM-free only.</strong> If a book has DRM applied — and that includes essentially all traditionally published bestsellers and most KDP Select exclusive titles — the EPUB download option is not available. You'll need to use the Kindle app or a Kindle device.</li>
  <li><strong>Author-facing tool.</strong> The clearest download path is through the KDP Author Dashboard. The reader-facing Content &amp; Devices page does have the option, but it's buried and inconsistent across accounts.</li>
  <li><strong>Format quality varies.</strong> The EPUB files Amazon generates are converted from the Kindle source format, not native EPUB builds. Some authors report formatting artifacts: missing drop caps, shifted margins, or embedded font references that don't resolve in non-Kindle readers.</li>
  <li><strong>No batch download.</strong> You must download books one at a time. For authors with 20+ titles, this is tedious.</li>
  <li><strong>Back catalog not retroactively converted.</strong> Only books published <em>after</em> the DRM policy change (or re-published with updated settings) get the EPUB download option. Older DRM-free titles may still show only the legacy Kindle format download.</li>
</ul>

