<p>Amazon remains the <strong>least EPUB-friendly</strong> major ebook retailer, even with this change. Every other retailer — Kobo, Google, Apple, Barnes &amp; Noble, Bookshop.org, and the new Readino store — offers native EPUB downloads as a standard feature, not an exception for DRM-free content only.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="what-this-means-for-self-publishers">What This Means for Self-Publishers</h2>

<p>If you are a self-published author who uses KDP, this change simplifies your workflow significantly:</p>

<h3 id="before-the-change">Before the Change</h3>
<p>You created your book in EPUB (the submission format KDP accepts), uploaded it to KDP, and then lost access to a clean EPUB download. To submit the same book to Kobo or Apple Books, you had to save a separate copy, manage version drift, or re-export from your original source. Many authors maintained multiple file versions manually.</p>

<h3 id="after-the-change">After the Change</h3>
<p>You upload your EPUB to KDP once. When you need an EPUB for another retailer, you download it from the KDP dashboard. The EPUB Amazon generates is, in most cases, the same file you uploaded (with minor reformatting for Kindle conversion). No version drift, no separate file management.</p>

<p><strong>Caveat for formatters:</strong> If your EPUB uses advanced CSS features or embedded fonts, the re-downloaded EPUB may have been altered by Amazon's conversion pipeline. Always re-download and test on an EPUB reader before submitting to another store. If the formatting is broken, you may still need to maintain a separate "master" EPUB file.</p>

