<hr />

<h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion: A Cautious Step Forward for Open Formats</h2>

<p>Amazon's decision to offer EPUB downloads for DRM-free KDP books is historically significant — it marks the first time the company has officially participated in the open EPUB ecosystem rather than fighting it. For self-published authors, it simplifies cross-platform distribution. For readers, it provides a legitimate, no-tools-required path to back up DRM-free purchases as standard EPUB files.</p>

<p>But the limitations are real. The change only covers DRM-free indie titles, which represent a meaningful but still minority share of Amazon's catalog. Readers who buy traditionally published books or Kindle Unlimited titles remain locked in. And Amazon still does not support EPUB on any Kindle device.</p>

<p>If you are a reader who cares about <strong>owning your books in an open, portable format</strong>, the best advice remains: buy EPUBs from retailers that treat EPUB as a first-class citizen — Kobo, Google Play Books, Apple Books, or the emerging Bookshop.org and Readino stores. Use Amazon exclusively for DRM-free indie purchases that you can download and archive as EPUB. Your future self — opening your library on whatever device exists in 2036 — will thank you.</p>

